3. TOP 5 DEEP DIVES #1 agent-mcp-gateway =E2=80=94 authority-aware dynamic
discovery

This is a particularly close Privately/Monid neighbour because it combines
context compression with explicit per-agent tool visibility. The key
security insight: discovery and authorization can be the same operation. A
subagent need not receive the existence/schema of tools it lacks authority
to invoke. Smallest experiment: put 3 synthetic agents behind it with
disjoint grants and test whether enumeration, schema retrieval and
execution remain non-interfering. Falsifier: identity is spoofable or
policy can be bypassed by omitted/forged agent_id.
#2 TMoW =E2=80=94 learned routing at test time

TMoW is closer to the JEV thesis than generic LLM routing: its router
changes at test time based on world-model simulation/prototype refinement.
This suggests QP can eventually separate expensive cognition from a cheap
learned policy over a bounded action/grant set. Smallest experiment:
reproduce the routing abstraction on a tiny synthetic authority task rather
than the full embodied stack. Falsifier: learned router adds no value over
calibrated static/rule routing under distribution shift.
#3 agent-opfor =E2=80=94 agent-native adversary emulation

Most scanners inspect artifacts; Opfor exercises an agent as a stateful
system across prompts, tools, memory and traces. That is much closer to the
Privately Lab objective. Integrate only against isolated toy agents and
compare findings to static MCP scanners. Falsifier: mostly generic prompt
libraries with little incremental detection over existing harnesses.
#4 toolfence =E2=80=94 temporal trust drift as a product primitive

Toolfence fingerprints tool definitions and alerts when tools
appear/disappear/change after trust. This directly validates the scanner=E2=
=80=99s
earlier TEMPORAL_TRUST_DRIFT hypothesis. Privately should generalize this
beyond MCP descriptors to principal, schema, dependencies, permissions,
network destinations and effects. Falsifier: real-world drift is too noisy
to distinguish benign updates without semantic/version provenance.
#5 OmniAgent =E2=80=94 bounded active perception

OmniAgent turns long-video understanding into explicit bounded actions (
get_frames, get_audio, get_clip, answer) and retains compressed textual
memory rather than raw media. This is both a JEV-like action-selection
object and potentially useful for the autonomous-media feedback loop: a
critic need not rewatch every frame. Falsifier: active perception misses
aesthetic/temporal details needed for video optimization.
